{
  "fileColumn": false,
  "stacked": true,
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "place",
      "title": "1. Declaring a place, and its kinds",
      "icon": "map",
      "description": "A program names where its data lives once. These are that one line's words."
    },
    {
      "id": "details",
      "title": "2. A place's details",
      "icon": "list check",
      "description": "Lines indented under the place line. A back-end that follows the usual conventions needs none of them."
    },
    {
      "id": "type",
      "title": "3. Saying where a type lives",
      "icon": "folder",
      "description": "One line in a type, and everything else stays as it is for things in memory."
    },
    {
      "id": "read",
      "title": "4. Reading",
      "icon": "search",
      "description": "Asking for data as if it were already here. Few new words:  most of these reach further than they did."
    },
    {
      "id": "show",
      "title": "5. Showing a read",
      "icon": "eye",
      "description": "The other three things a read can come to, written right under the loop."
    },
    {
      "id": "change",
      "title": "6. Changing",
      "icon": "pen",
      "description": "The same words as any list, and now they keep the change.  Plus the rare Edit screen, and undo."
    },
    {
      "id": "named",
      "title": "7. Named operations",
      "icon": "paper plane",
      "description": "Something a place can do that isn't add, change or remove.  This section answers the open question on the epic page."
    },
    {
      "id": "people",
      "title": "8. Several people",
      "icon": "users",
      "description": "Who's here, and doing something about a change.  All of it still being built."
    },
    {
      "id": "sample",
      "title": "9. A sample, in the side bar",
      "icon": "flask",
      "description": "Not a word in the language:  what the switch says while someone is designing."
    }
  ],
